echo '<div class="mk-prose">';
echo '<h2>Leaders, Thinkers, Resisters, and Chroniclers</h2>';
echo '<h3>Olaudah Equiano (c.1745-1797)</h3>';
echo '<p>Olaudah Equiano is the most significant Igbo voice in the historical record before the 20th century. Born in what is now Anambra State, kidnapped and enslaved as a child, transported across the Atlantic, sold multiple times, and eventually purchasing his own freedom, Equiano settled in Britain and became one of the leading voices of the 18th-century abolitionist movement. His autobiography — <em>The Interesting Narrative of the Life of Olaudah Equiano</em> (1789) — is simultaneously the founding document of Igbo diaspora literature, the most important Black abolitionist text of its era, and an invaluable source on 18th-century Igbo life. He describes Igbo village life, its social institutions, its moral culture, and its religious practices with the precision of someone who lived it. He remains the Igbo voice through which the world first encountered the Igbo.</p>';
echo '<h3>Nnamdi Azikiwe (1904-1996)</h3>';
echo '<p>Nnamdi Azikiwe — "Zik of Africa" — was the towering figure of Nigerian nationalism and the most significant Igbo political figure of the 20th century\'s first half. Educated in the United States, he returned to West Africa as a journalist and politician, founding the West African Pilot newspaper in 1937, the National Council of Nigeria and the Cameroons (NCNC) political party, and eventually becoming the first President of Nigeria (1963-1966). His pan-African vision — articulated in works like <em>Renascent Africa</em> (1937) — connected the Igbo national experience to the broader African and diaspora struggle for dignity and self-determination. His displacement by the military coups of 1966 removed from Nigerian politics its most significant proponent of genuine federalism and inter-ethnic solidarity.</p>';
echo '<h3>Odumegwu Ojukwu (1933-2011)</h3>';
echo '<p>Chukwuemeka Odumegwu Ojukwu is the most contested figure in modern Igbo history. The son of one of Nigeria\'s wealthiest men, educated at Epsom College and Lincoln College Oxford, he became Military Governor of Eastern Nigeria after the July 1966 counter-coup and the leader who declared Biafran independence on 30 May 1967. His decision — taken after the failure of the Aburi Accord and in the context of mass killings of Igbo in the north — was supported by the overwhelming majority of the Igbo population. He led the Biafran war effort for two and a half years against overwhelming odds, fled to Côte d\'Ivoire in January 1970, was pardoned in 1982, returned to Nigeria, and spent the remainder of his life advocating for Igbo interests within the federal system. He died in London in 2011 and was buried with full military honours.</p>';
echo '<h3>Chinua Achebe (1930-2013)</h3>';
echo '<p>Chinua Achebe is the most significant Igbo cultural figure of the 20th century and, by most measures, the most significant African writer of any century. Born in Ogidi, Anambra State, his novel <em>Things Fall Apart</em> (1958) — which sold over 20 million copies in 57 languages — made the precolonial Igbo world fully visible to a global audience in all its complexity, beauty, and tragedy. His subsequent novels documented the colonial encounter and its aftermath with equal rigour. His essay "An Image of Africa: Racism in Conrad\'s Heart of Darkness" (1977) remains the most important critique of Eurocentric representations of Africa in the English language. His memoir <em>There Was a Country</em> (2012) gave the definitive Igbo literary account of the Biafra War.</p>';
echo '<h3>Flora Nwapa (1931-1993)</h3>';
echo '<p>Flora Nwapa was the first African woman to be published by a major international publisher — Heinemann\'s African Writers Series published <em>Efuru</em> in 1966 — and the first African woman to establish her own publishing house (Tana Press, 1977). Born in Oguta, Rivers State, she drew on the women\'s oral traditions of the Igbo lakeshore communities to create fiction that centred Igbo women\'s interior lives, spiritual relationships, and social conflicts in ways that had never before appeared in African literature. She is underacknowledged relative to her significance, in part because she operated outside the male-dominated networks of the African Writers Series and international literary prize culture.</p>';
echo '<h3>Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie (b.1977)</h3>';
echo '<p>Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie is the most globally significant Igbo writer of the 21st century. Born in Enugu and raised in Nsukka in the house previously occupied by Chinua Achebe, her novels — <em>Purple Hibiscus</em> (2003), <em>Half of a Yellow Sun</em> (2006), <em>Americanah</em> (2013) — have been translated into over 30 languages and read by tens of millions. Her TED talks — "The Danger of a Single Story" (2009) and "We Should All Be Feminists" (2012) — have reached hundreds of millions of viewers. She is the inheritor and extender of the Achebe tradition: the writer who makes the Igbo world visible to the world on its own terms.</p>';
